"Spherical Steering: Geometry-Aware Activation Rotation for Language Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.08169v1)\nciteturn31642view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn31639view0\",\"lineno\":255}); Total lines: 533\nL231: ##### Models.\nL232: \nL233: We evaluate on Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct (cite91†Yang et al., 2024 ) and LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct (cite60†Dubey et al., 2024 ), two state-of-the-art open-source instruction-tuned models with distinct architectural characteristics.\nL234: ##### Baselines.\nL235: We compare against representative activation steering methods: (1) Inference-Time Intervention (ITI) (cite79†Li et al., 2023 ), which uses linear probes to identify steering heads; (2) Contrastive Activation Addition (CAA) (cite80†Rimsky et al., 2024 ), which applies layer-wise additive interventions; (3) SADI-HEAD (cite102†Wang et al., 2024 ), which uses dynamic element-wise scaling at attention heads; and (4) 5-shot In-Context Learning (ICL) as a prompt-only baseline.\nL236: All methods use identical data splits and undergo dedicated hyperparameter sweeps for fair comparison.\nL237: Table 1: TruthfulQA results on LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct and Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct. Metrics are in percentage (%). Key insight: Spherical Steering achieves the best multiple-choice performance while simultaneously maintaining or improving generation quality—a Pareto improvement over all baselines.\nL238: Model  | Method  | Multiple-Choice  | Open-ended Generation\nL239: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL240:  | MC1  | MC2  | MC3  | Avg.  | TRUE  | INFO  | TRUE$\\times$INFO\nL241: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL242: LLaMA- 3.1-8B- Instruct  | Baseline  | 34.15  | 53.32  | 27.02  | 38.16  | 82.32  | 58.60  | 48.24\nL243:  | 5-shot ICL  | 38.31  | 57.85  | 30.09  | 42.08  | 89.17  | 43.66  | 38.93\nL244:  | ITI (cite79†Li et al., 2023 )  | 37.70  | 58.09  | 30.12  | 41.97  | 89.66  | 44.96  | 40.31\nL245:  | CAA (cite80†Rimsky et al., 2024 )  | 35.99  | 56.26  | 29.36  | 40.54  | 84.63  | 58.67  | 49.66\nL246:  | SADI-HEAD (cite102†Wang et al., 2024 )  | 38.53  | 56.03  | 30.57  | 41.71  | 80.30  | 63.74  | 51.18\nL247:  | Spherical Steering (Ours)  | 49.95  | 68.51  | 41.05  | 53.17  | 82.05  | 66.57  | 54.63\nL248: Qwen- 2.5-7B- Instruct  | Baseline  | 35.87  | 54.95  | 26.62  | 39.15  | 83.65  | 88.93  | 74.40\nL249:  | 5-shot ICL  | 43.33  | 63.36  | 32.15  | 46.28  | 78.39  | 97.99  | 76.94\nL250:  | ITI (cite79†Li et al., 2023 )  | 40.15  | 58.93  | 30.26  | 43.11  | 99.60  | 68.10  | 67.82\nL251:  | CAA (cite80†Rimsky et al., 2024 )  | 40.16  | 59.08  | 30.41  | 43.22  | 98.99  | 68.63  | 67.95\nL252:  | SADI-HEAD (cite102†Wang et al., 2024 )  | 39.28  | 56.61  | 29.32  | 41.74  | 97.00  | 71.23  | 69.09\nL253:  | Spherical Steering (Ours)  | 48.71  | 66.90  | 39.16  | 51.59  | 88.02  | 88.44  | 77.84\nL254: ### 4.2 Main Results: Pareto-Optimal Accuracy Generation Trade-off\nL255: Table cite93†1 presents our primary results on TruthfulQA. Spherical Steering dominates all baselines in multiple-choice performance across both model families, achieving MC average improvements of +11.09% on LLaMA and +5.31% on Qwen over the best baseline. Remarkably, these accuracy gains do not come at the expense of generation quality—our method achieves the highest TRUE$\\times$INFO scores (+3.45% and +0.90% respectively), demonstrating a Pareto improvement over activation addition methods.\nL256: The contrast with additive baselines is particularly striking: ITI and CAA can boost MC accuracy but consistently degrade TRUE$\\times$INFO (e.g., ITI drops from 48.24$\\to$40.31 on LLaMA), revealing a fundamental trade-off inherent to magnitude-altering interventions. In contrast, our norm-preserving rotation simultaneously enhances both decision-making precision and generation fluency, validating our core hypothesis that respecting geometric structure enables robust control without manifold degradation.\nL257: Table 2: LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct results (MC accuracy, %) across multiple-choice benchmarks, including TruthfulQA (MC1). All methods are evaluated zero-shot. Avg denotes the mean of all benchmarks.\nL258: Method  | TruthfulQA  | COPA  | StoryCloze  | MMLU  | Wino.  | BoolQ  | Avg.\nL259: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL260: Baseline  | 34.15  | 83.00  | 74.72  | 60.60  | 50.81  | 80.12  | 63.90\nL261: ITI (cite79†Li et al., 2023 )  | 37.70  | 83.00  | 75.12  | 60.90  | 51.85  | 81.53  | 65.02\nL262: CAA (cite80†Rimsky et al., 2024 )  | 35.99  | 84.00  | 79.02  | 60.70  | 51.93  | 82.42  | 65.68\nL263: SADI-HEAD (cite102†Wang et al., 2024 )  | 38.53  | 84.00  | 75.72  | 60.66  | 51.85  | 80.20  | 65.16\nL264: Spherical Steering (Ours)  | 49.95  | 95.00  | 89.08  | 62.05  | 52.72  | 82.94  | 71.96\nL265: ### 4.3 Generalization Across Reasoning Benchmarks\nL266: To assess generalization beyond truthfulness, we evaluate on five additional multiple-choice benchmarks spanning commonsense reasoning (COPA, StoryCloze, WinoGrande), world knowledge (MMLU), and reading comprehension (BoolQ). As shown in Table cite123†2 , Spherical Steering achieves best performance on all six benchmarks, with particularly dramatic improvements on tasks requiring nuanced semantic discrimination: +11.00% on COPA (plausible alternative selection) and +10.06% on StoryCloze (narrative coherence).\nL267: These gains highlight that norm-preserving rotation effectively sharpens the model’s ability to distinguish between similar-but-distinct alternatives—a capability that additive methods struggle to achieve without degrading the representation manifold.\nL268: Figure 3: Activation-norm analysis on TruthfulQA for LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct. Top: mean $\\ell_{2}$ norm of last-token activations at each residual layer for correct (denoted as Truthful) vs. incorrect answers (denoted as Hallucinated), shaded areas indicate mean$\\pm$std. The curves nearly overlap, indicating similar activation magnitudes of the same layer. Bottom: $\\Delta\\text{Norm}$, defined as the mean norm of correct answers minus that of incorrect answers at each layer.\nL269: ### 4.4 Analysis: Why Norm Preservation Matters\nL270: \nL271: To understand the superior performance of Spherical Steering, we first provide empirical evidence that truthfulness is encoded in activation direction rather than magnitude, validating our hyperspherical geometry assumption. We then analyze the collapse-efficiency trade-off, demonstrating why norm-preserving rotation achieves stronger performance gains with less representational degradation compared to additive methods.\nL272: #### 4.4.1 Direction vs. Magnitude: Empirical Validation of Hyperspherical Geometry\nL273: We provide direct empirical evidence that truthfulness is encoded directionally rather than in activation magnitudes. Figure cite124†3 shows the mean $\\ell_{2}$ norms of last-token activations across all layers on TruthfulQA, separately for truthful vs. hallucinated answers. The curves nearly perfectly overlap across all 32 layers, with negligible differences ($<$1% relative deviation).\nL274: This striking result validates two key claims: (1) activation magnitude alone is non-discriminative for separating truthful from hallucinated behaviors at a given layer, and (2) the semantic/behavioral signal must therefore reside primarily in the directional components. Consequently, a norm-preserving directional update—as implemented by Spherical Steering—aligns more naturally with this geometric structure than unconstrained additive shifts that arbitrarily alter magnitudes.\nL275: #### 4.4.2 Representation Collapse Analysis: Collapse-Efficiency Trade-off\nL276: To understand why norm-preserving rotation outperforms additive steering, we further analyze the collapse-efficiency trade-off: how much performance gain each method achieves per unit of representational degradation.\nL277: We measure representation collapse using effective rank (computed from the singular value spectrum of the stacked token-activation matrix) and sweep intervention strength for both Spherical Steering (ungated) and activation addition across ten increasing values (detailed settings in Appendix cite47†C.3 ).\nL278: (a) Multiple-choice accuracy vs. efficient rank drop.\nL279: \nL280: (b) Open-ended generation quality vs. efficient rank drop.\nL281: \nL282: Figure 4: Analysis of efficient rank on LLaMA-3.1-8B-Insturct. We sweep intervention strength of two types of steering mechanism. Spherical Steering achieves much larger performance gains than addition steering at comparable rank drop.\nL283: Figure cite125†4 reveals a fundamental difference in how the two geometric operations convert representation collapse into downstream gains. For multiple-choice accuracy (Fig. cite126†4(a) ), activation addition yields modest gains that peak early and then decline as rank drops further, whereas Spherical Steering achieves markedly higher accuracy throughout the range. Critically, at matched levels of rank drop (e.g., $\\Delta$rank $\\approx$ 50), rotation delivers 8-10% higher MC accuracy than addition.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPretraining with Token-Level Adaptive Latent Chain-of-Thought (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.08220v1)\nciteturn31642view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn31639view1\",\"lineno\":175}); Total lines: 343\nL157: ### 3.4 Correctness-Aware Adaptive Loss\nL158: \nL159: While probabilistic halting enables token-wise adaptive computation, we additionally need to guide the Router to stop when extra computation is unlikely to help. As evidenced in cite79†Figure 2 , once $p_{\\mathrm{target}}^{(k)}$ is high, additional latent steps bring little benefit and can even be detrimental. We therefore propose a Correctness-Aware Adaptive Loss that encourages early halting for tokens that are already likely correct, reducing overall FLOPs. Let\nL160:  | $$p_{\\mathrm{target},t}^{(k)}\\triangleq\\bigl[\\mathrm{LMHead}(z_{t}^{(k)})\\bigr]_{y_{t}}$$  |  | (9)\nL161: \nL162: denote the probability assigned to the ground-truth token $y_{t}$ at step $k$. We encourage the model to halt when it is already likely correct by penalizing continuation proportional to this step-wise target probability:\nL163: \nL164:  | $$\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{adaptive}}=\\lambda\\sum_{t}\\sum_{k=1}^{K_{t}^{\\star}}g_{t}^{(k)}\\cdot\\mathrm{sg}\\!\\left((p_{\\mathrm{target},t}^{(k)})^{\\beta}\\right),$$  |  | (10)\nL165: where $\\mathrm{sg}(\\cdot)$ denotes stop-gradient to prevent degenerate solutions (e.g., reducing $p_{\\mathrm{target}}$ solely to weaken the compute penalty), $\\beta$ controls the nonlinearity of the weighting (larger $\\beta$ concentrates the penalty on high-$p_{\\mathrm{target}}$ steps), and $\\lambda$ scales the overall strength of the adaptive term relative to $\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{CE}}$.\nL166: Intuitively, when $p_{\\mathrm{target}}^{(k)}$ is high, the penalty encourages halting ($g\\!\\to\\!0$); when the model is uncertain, the penalty is small, allowing additional latent computation.\nL167: The overall objective is:\nL168: \nL169:  | $$\\mathcal{L}=\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{CE}}\\!\\Bigl(\\mathrm{LMHead}(z^{\\mathrm{final}}),y\\Bigr)+\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{adaptive}}.$$  |  | (11)\nL170: ### 3.5 Training and Inference Workflows\nL171: \nL172: We now summarize how the above components are integrated in practice.\nL173: cite81†Image: Refer to caption Figure 3: Inference: token-wise adaptive latent CoT via reach probability. Left: At decoding position $t$, the model iterates latent steps to produce $z_{t}^{(1)},z_{t}^{(2)},\\ldots$ and updates the reach probability, stopping when the next-step reach probability $p_{\\mathrm{reach},t}^{(k+1)}$ falls below the threshold $\\tau$. Easy tokens ($x_{2},x_{3}$) stop earlier (shorter latent CoT), while harder tokens ($x_{1}$) continue longer.\nL174: The final representation is obtained by $p_{\\mathrm{exit}}$-weighted mixing of the executed latent states, and is fed to the LM head for prediction. Right: The Router takes each latent hidden state $z_{t}^{(k)}$ and outputs a gate $g_{t}^{(k)}$ (the conditional continuation probability), which defines the probability flow: $p_{\\mathrm{reach}}^{k+1}=p_{\\mathrm{reach}}^{k}g^{k}$ and $p_{\\mathrm{exit}}^{k}=p_{\\mathrm{reach}}^{k}(1-g^{k})$.\nL175: ##### Training.\nL176: cite82†Image: Refer to caption Figure 4: A training example with unrolled latent steps, pruning, and KV-cache reuse. We illustrate one sequence $(x_{1},x_{2},x_{3})$ with a latent budget $K_{\\max}=4$. With the parallel attention mask, we unroll computation along latent steps and, at each step, compute all active tokens in parallel.\nL177: At step 1, the model produces $(z_{1}^{(1)},z_{2}^{(1)},z_{3}^{(1)})$ and the Router outputs gates (conditional continuation probabilities) for each token, which update $p_{\\mathrm{reach}}$ and determine whether a token remains active. In the figure, $x_{1},x_{2}$ keep sufficient reach probability to proceed (green checks), while $x_{3}$ is pruned (red cross). At step 2, only $x_{1},x_{2}$ remain; the updated reach probability prunes $x_{2}$, so steps 3–4 are executed only for $x_{1}$.\nL178: Across latent steps, we reuse the KV cache so later steps reuse cached attention context, avoiding redundant computation. Since pruning shrinks the active token set as $k$ increases, the overall training FLOPs decrease.\nL179: For supervision, we compute per-position LM losses ($\\mathcal{L}_{1},\\mathcal{L}_{2},\\mathcal{L}_{3}$) on the final representation $z_{t}^{\\mathrm{f}}$ obtained by $p_{\\mathrm{exit}}$-weighted mixing of the executed latent states, and add a correctness-aware adaptive loss (proportional to $p_{\\mathrm{target}}$) to penalize continuing when the model already assigns high probability to the ground-truth token, the $p_{\\mathrm{exit}}$-weighted mixing and $\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{adaptive}}$ provide learning signals to both the LM and the Router.\nL180: We unroll latent computation for $k=1,\\ldots,K_{\\max}$ under the parallel attention mask. At each latent step, all active token positions are processed in parallel over $t$. To improve efficiency, we reuse the KV cache across latent steps to avoid recomputing shared attention context, and apply threshold pruning so the active token set shrinks as $k$ increases, reducing overall training FLOPs (cite83†Figure 4 ).\nL181: The Router is trained jointly through (i) gradients from the main cross-entropy loss computed on the mixed representation $z^{\\mathrm{final}}$ and (ii) the correctness-aware adaptive penalty that discourages unnecessary continuation.\nL182: ##### Inference.\nL183: \nL184: We use the same Router to perform token-wise halting during decoding. For each position, we iteratively compute latent states and update the reach probability, stopping when the next-step reach probability falls below a threshold $\\tau$. We then form $z^{\\mathrm{final}}$ via the (truncated) $p_{\\mathrm{exit}}$-weighted mixing of the executed latent states and emit the next token with the LM head (cite84†Figure 3 ).\nL185: ### 3.6 Positional Embedding\nL186: \nL187: All latent-step states $z_{t}^{(k)}$ share the same positional embedding (position id) as the input token $x_{t}$ at position $t$, rather than using new positions. Thus, latent steps do not consume extra position ids, and the effective context window stays unchanged from the vanilla model.\nL188: \nL189: ## 4 Experiments\nL190: \nL191: ### 4.1 Setup\nL192: ##### Training Setup and Baselines.\nL193: We train all models from scratch using the LLaMA architecture (cite85†Dubey et al., 2024 ). Specifically, we pretrain two model sizes (410M and 1.4B parameters) for 26B tokens on the Pile over 50,000 optimization steps, following the protocol of cite57†Zeng et al. (2025a) .\nL194: To ensure a controlled comparison, we train a suite of baselines under an identical pretraining protocol—matching the training data, optimization schedule, and hyperparameters—including vanilla LLaMA models as well as competitive compute-scaling and recurrent variants: LoopedLM (cite55†Giannou et al., 2023 ), PausedLM (cite67†Goyal et al., 2023 ), PonderLM (cite54†Zeng et al., 2025b ), PonderLM2 (cite57†Zeng et al., 2025a ), and the adaptive Mixture-of-Recurrence (MoR) method (cite66†Bae et al., 2025 ).\nL195: cite86†Image: Refer to caption Figure 5: Language modeling perplexity (PPL $\\downarrow$). Validation perplexity on The Pile, WikiText, and LAMBADA (OpenAI and standard splits). Our method consistently achieves the lowest perplexity across all datasets while using fewer training FLOPs.\nL196: ##### Evaluation.\nL197: We evaluate language modeling quality using validation perplexity on The Pile (cite87†Gao et al., 2020 ), WikiText (cite88†Merity et al., 2016 ), and LAMBADA (cite89†Paperno et al., 2016 ) (both the OpenAI and standard splits).\nL198: To assess downstream generalization, we follow the evaluation protocol of cite90†Gu and Dao (2024) and report 0-shot and 5-shot accuracy on a broad benchmark suite, including LAMBADA (cite89†Paperno et al., 2016 ), SciQ (cite91†Welbl et al., 2017 ), HellaSwag (cite92†Zellers et al., 2019 ), PIQA (cite93†Bisk et al., 2020 ), WinoGrande (cite94†Sakaguchi et al., 2021 ), ARC-Easy and ARC-Challenge (cite95†Clark et al., 2018 ), and RACE (cite96†Lai et al., 2017 ).\nL199: ### 4.2 Main Results\nL200: \nL201: ##### Language Modeling Ability.\nL202: \nL203: As shown in cite97†Figure 5 , our method consistently achieves the lowest perplexity across all datasets while using the least training FLOPs. Specifically, our LLaMA-1.4B model with max latent CoT length $\\ell_{\\max}=3$ outperforms the strongest baseline (PonderLM-2) while requiring less than half of the training compute (7.47 vs. 17.47 $\\times 10^{20}$ FLOPs). Moreover, increasing $\\ell_{\\max}$ to 5 further improves performance.\nL204: ##### Downstream Tasks.\nL205: \nL206: Across both zero-shot and five-shot settings and under all compute budgets, our method achieves the best overall average accuracy (cite98†Table 1 ). Notably, our LLaMA-410M with $\\ell_{\\max}=3$ outperforms the compute-comparable vanilla LLaMA-1.4B baseline in average accuracy for both zero-shot and five-shot, indicating that our approach can be even more effective than simply scaling up the parameter count under a matched compute budget.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPretraining with Token-Level Adaptive Latent Chain-of-Thought (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.08220v1)\nciteturn31642view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn31639view1\",\"lineno\":249}); Total lines: 343\nL230: LLaMA-1.4B (train from scratch)^{†}  | 2.18  | 45.1  | 34.7  | 22.3  | 53.5  | 50.9  | 66.1  | 33.6  | 89.3  | 31.6  | 47.5\nL231: Our LLaMA–410M ($\\ell_{\\max}=3$)  | 2.27  | 42.7  | 35.7  | 23.6  | 53.8  | 52.3  | 67.3  | 33.7  | 89.8  | 32.0  | 47.9\nL232: Looped LLaMA-1.4B (4 loops)  | 8.74  | 48.0  | 42.8  | 25.0  | 58.5  | 54.8  | 70.4  | 37.6  | 89.2  | 28.5  | 50.5\nL233: Pause LLaMA-1.4B (3 pauses)  | 8.74  | 48.8  | 41.0  | 24.1  | 56.6  | 54.1  | 69.3  | 36.6  | 90.7  | 32.6  | 50.4\nL234: PonderLM-1.4B (3 steps)  | 8.74  | 49.5  | 42.6  | 25.4  | 58.8  | 54.3  | 69.2  | 37.9  | 91.2  | 34.7  | 51.5\nL235: Our LLaMA-1.4B ($\\ell_{\\max}=3$)  | 7.47  | 51.7  | 41.7  | 27.7  | 60.4  | 56.0  | 70.4  | 39.6  | 92.4  | 32.7  | 52.5\nL236: PonderLM-1.4B (4 steps)  | 10.92  | 50.3  | 44.5  | 26.0  | 58.6  | 55.8  | 69.9  | 38.4  | 92.4  | 33.1  | 52.1\nL237: PonderLM2-1.4B (1 step)  | 17.47  | 49.8  | 45.6  | 27.7  | 59.6  | 56.3  | 69.8  | 38.7  | 91.3  | 28.0  | 51.9\nL238: Our LLaMA-1.4B ($\\ell_{\\max}=5$)  | 10.84  | 54.8  | 51.0  | 27.5  | 62.5  | 56.8  | 71.4  | 41.2  | 91.4  | 35.5  | 54.7\nL239: ### 4.3 Iso-data and Iso-FLOP Comparison\nL240: \nL241: cite99†Image: Refer to caption Figure 6: Validation loss versus token and compute. Our method consistently achieves the lowest validation loss across budgets.\nL242: \nL243: cite100†Image: Refer to caption Figure 7: Case studies of our adaptive latent cot model. Two examples from the 1.4B model with $l_{\\max}=5$, showing per-token executed latent steps (color-coded).\nL244: In cite101†Figure 6 , we compare four 410M-parameter models (LoopedLM, PausedLM, PonderLM, and ours) and report their validation loss under two alignment settings: (i) iso-data, where checkpoints are matched by the same token/step budget, and (ii) iso-FLOPs, where checkpoints are matched by the same estimated total training compute. Across both settings and all budgets, our method consistently achieves the lowest validation loss.\nL245: ### 4.4 Analysis of Token-Level Adaptive Latent CoT\nL246: We analyze when the model decides to halt or continue latent CoT, and how these decisions allocate computation across tokens. We use our previously trained LLaMA-1.4B models with max latent CoT length $\\ell_{\\max}\\in\\{3,5\\}$.\nL247: Specifically, we study: (i) the relationship between token-wise latent CoT length and $p_{\\text{target}}$ (the probability assigned to the ground-truth next token), (ii) whether the model generates shorter latent CoT for easy tokens and longer latent CoT for difficult tokens, and (iii) qualitative case studies illustrating where longer latent CoT is triggered in real sequences.\nL248: #### 4.4.1 Token-wise Latent CoT Length vs. $p_{\\text{target}}$\nL249: We examine how $p_{\\text{target}}$ varies across tokens that execute different latent CoT lengths. The results in cite102†Figure 8 exhibit a clear monotonic trend: tokens with longer latent CoT tend to have lower $p_{\\text{target}}$, whereas tokens with higher $p_{\\text{target}}$ typically halt earlier. This observation aligns with our correctness-aware design: pruning is encouraged when the ground-truth token already receives a high probability, while ambiguous tokens are allocated more latent computation.\nL250: #### 4.4.2 Token Difficulty vs. Latent CoT Length\nL251: On WikiText (cite88†Merity et al., 2016 ), we use token-wise cross-entropy as a difficulty score and bucket tokens accordingly. As shown in cite102†Figure 8 , latent CoT length increases monotonically with difficulty: easy (low-loss) tokens execute $\\sim$0–1 latent steps on average, while difficult (high-loss) tokens trigger deeper computation and approach the maximum length.\nL252: This suggests the model learns a sensible compute-allocation rule, using minimal latent CoT for easy tokens and longer latent CoT for difficult ones.\nL253: cite103†Image: Refer to caption L254: \nL255: Figure 8: Left: $p_{\\text{target}}$ monotonically decreases with latent CoT length: tokens that generate longer latent CoT have lower $p_{\\text{target}}$. Right: We bucket tokens by token-wise cross-entropy (log-scale) and report average latent COT length for 1.4B models with $l_{\\max}\\in\\{3,5\\}$. Easy tokens use $\\sim$0–1 steps, while harder tokens receive more computation.\nL256: #### 4.4.3 Case Studies\nL257: Finally, we visualize where the model chooses to generate long latent CoT in real sequences. cite104†Figure 7 shows two examples from the 1.4B model with max latent CoT length $\\ell_{\\max}=5$, where each token is colored by the number of executed latent steps. The router assigns minimal computation to highly predictable spans (e.g., common function words and locally determined continuations), while allocating longer latent CoT to information-dense tokens such as entities, numbers, and key content words.\nL258: ## 5 Ablation Studies\nL259: \nL260: We run controlled ablations on 70M-parameter Llama models to isolate key design choices in our adaptive latent CoT.\nL261: ### 5.1 Effect of Router Architecture.\nL262: \nL263: We compare five router designs with the backbone and training setup fixed (cite105†Table 2 ). No-Adaptive disables routing (no pruning). Linear/MLP share one router across latent steps, while Multi-Linear/Multi-MLP use step-specific routers (no sharing). As shown in cite105†Table 2 , adaptive routing improves efficiency while also improving performance. The shared Linear router achieves the lowest CE loss, and we use it as the default.\nL264: Table 2: Effect of router architecture ($l_{\\max}=5$). No-Adaptive disables routing (no pruning). Linear/MLP share one router across latent steps, while Multi-Linear/Multi-MLP use step-specific routers. We report CE loss (lower is better) and prune ratio (higher saves more compute). Notably, shared Linear outperforms No-Adaptive in CE loss while pruning $\\sim$12% of steps.\nL265: Router  | $\\lambda$  | $\\beta$  | CE $\\downarrow$  | Prune Ratio (%) $\\uparrow$\nL266: No-Adaptive  | –  | –  | 2.671  | –\nL267: MLP  | 0.4  | 10  | 2.675  | 13.4\nL268: Linear  | 0.4  | 10  | 2.670  | 12.4\nL269: Multi-MLP  | 0.4  | 10  | 2.672  | 13.8\nL270: Multi-Linear  | 0.4  | 10  | 2.672  | 11.8\nL271: ### 5.2 Effect of $\\lambda$ and $\\beta$ in the Adaptive Loss.\nL272: We ablate the two key hyperparameters in our correctness-aware adaptive loss (cite106†Equation 10 ): the compute weight $\\lambda$ and the exponent $\\beta$ on $p_{\\text{target}}$. All runs use a shared linear router with $l_{\\max}{=}5$; when varying $\\lambda$ we fix $\\beta{=}10$, and when varying $\\beta$ we fix $\\lambda{=}0.4$.\nL273: As shown in cite107†Figure 9 (a), larger $\\lambda$ increases the prune ratio but slightly worsens CE loss, reflecting a quality–efficiency trade-off; meanwhile cite107†Figure 9 (b) shows that larger $\\beta$ improves CE loss but reduces pruning, indicating more conservative early exits focused on highly confident tokens. We adopt $\\lambda{=}0.4$ and $\\beta{=}10$ in our experiments.\nL274: cite108†Image: Refer to caption Figure 9: Ablations on loss hyperparameters and max latent cot length $l_{\\max}$. Blue curves (left axis) report CE loss (lower is better), and red curves (right axis) report prune ratio (higher indicates more pruning), defined as $1-\\frac{\\mathbb{E}[\\ell]}{l_{\\max}}$, where $\\ell$ denotes the executed latent CoT length per token. (a) Vary $\\lambda$ with $\\beta{=}10$ and $l_{\\max}{=}5$. (b) Vary $\\beta$ with $\\lambda{=}0.4$ and $l_{\\max}{=}5$.\nL275: (c) Vary $l_{\\max}$ with $\\lambda{=}0.4$ and $\\beta{=}10$.\nL276: ### 5.3 Effect of max latent CoT length $l_{\\max}$.\nL277: \nL278: We vary $l_{\\max}$ and evaluate its impact on CE loss and pruning. As shown in cite107†Figure 9 (c), larger $l_{\\max}$ consistently reduces CE loss, suggesting extra latent cot improves modeling quality. Meanwhile, the prune ratio also increases, indicating the router uses the larger budget to skip more steps on easy tokens while allocating computation to difficult ones.\nL279: ## 6 Conclusion\nL280: \nL281: We introduced Adaptive Latent COT, a one-stage pretraining framework that increases per-token computation by generating variable-length latent COT before emitting each token. Experiments on LLaMA backbones show consistent improvements in language-modeling perplexity and downstream tasks compared to strong compute-scaling baselines.\nL282: ## Acknowledgement\nL283: \nL284: This work is sponsored by the National Natural Science Foundation of China (NSFC) grant (No. 62576211) and the National Key Research and Development Program of China (No. 2023ZD0121402).\nL285: ## References\n"
